1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,741
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,741 --> 00:00:07,149
This is chapter thirty-two: structured concurrency.

3
00:00:07,599 --> 00:00:12,390
It does for concurrent tasks what blocks and braces did for goto.

4
00:00:12,390 --> 00:00:16,887
A scope can't be exited while its subtasks are still running.

5
00:00:16,887 --> 00:00:24,110
One warning first: this is still a preview API, and its shape changed between Java 21 and Java 25.

6
00:00:24,110 --> 00:00:29,786
Check signatures against your JDK before copying any sample, including these.

7
00:00:30,686 --> 00:00:36,333
Loading an order context needs three independent lookups, and all must succeed.

8
00:00:36,333 --> 00:00:41,694
The usual approach submits three tasks to an executor and gets each result.

9
00:00:41,694 --> 00:00:42,695
It looks fine.

10
00:00:42,695 --> 00:00:44,339
Every line is a hazard.

11
00:00:44,789 --> 00:00:52,171
If stock fails, pricing and profile keep running to completion, wasting their load on downstream services.

12
00:00:52,621 --> 00:00:56,013
The executor is never shut down on the exception path.

13
00:00:56,463 --> 00:01:00,879
Results are fetched in order, so a pricing failure is discovered last.

14
00:01:01,329 --> 00:01:06,299
And at runtime, nothing records that these three threads belong to one request.

15
00:01:07,199 --> 00:01:09,629
A scope is a block with a lifetime.

16
00:01:09,629 --> 00:01:11,850
Open it in a try-with-resources.

17
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:14,241
Fork the subtasks inside it.

18
00:01:14,691 --> 00:01:18,041
join waits until the scope's policy is satisfied.

19
00:01:18,491 --> 00:01:22,386
And close is guaranteed, cancelling anything still running.

20
00:01:22,386 --> 00:01:26,875
The opening brace starts concurrency, and the closing brace ends it.

21
00:01:27,325 --> 00:01:40,039
Compared to executors and CompletableFuture, the lifetime is lexically scoped, siblings are cancelled automatically on failure, stack traces are complete, and the code reads sequentially.

22
00:01:40,939 --> 00:01:45,064
Here's the loader, structured, using the Java 25 shape.

23
00:01:45,064 --> 00:01:49,938
Open a scope with a joiner that requires all subtasks to succeed.

24
00:01:49,938 --> 00:01:51,438
Fork three subtasks.

25
00:01:51,438 --> 00:01:56,463
join returns when they all succeed, or throws on the first failure.

26
00:01:56,463 --> 00:01:58,113
Then read the results.

27
00:01:58,563 --> 00:02:04,351
If pricing fails at forty milliseconds, stock and profile are cancelled immediately.

28
00:02:04,351 --> 00:02:08,003
The scope closes on every path, including exceptions.

29
00:02:08,003 --> 00:02:13,585
The subtasks are virtual threads, and a thread dump groups them under this scope.

30
00:02:13,585 --> 00:02:15,928
And it reads like sequential code.

31
00:02:16,828 --> 00:02:18,467
The policy is a Joiner.

32
00:02:18,467 --> 00:02:24,593
allSuccessfulOrThrow: everything must succeed, and the first failure cancels the rest.

33
00:02:25,043 --> 00:02:30,462
anySuccessfulResultOrThrow: the first success wins, and the others are cancelled.

34
00:02:30,912 --> 00:02:36,949
awaitAll: wait for every subtask without throwing, and inspect each outcome yourself.

35
00:02:37,399 --> 00:02:41,474
And allUntil, with a predicate, for collecting partial results.

36
00:02:42,374 --> 00:02:44,654
Take the fastest of three vendors.

37
00:02:44,654 --> 00:02:49,012
Fork a quote request to each, and join returns the first success.

38
00:02:49,012 --> 00:02:51,425
The losers are cancelled mid-flight.

39
00:02:51,425 --> 00:02:57,862
With CompletableFuture dot anyOf, you'd get the first result, but the others would keep running.

40
00:02:58,312 --> 00:03:01,933
And for required plus optional work, use awaitAll.

41
00:03:01,933 --> 00:03:04,757
Join waits for both and throws nothing.

42
00:03:04,757 --> 00:03:13,373
Then decide per subtask: a missing stock status is an error, but missing recommendations just degrade to an empty list.

43
00:03:14,273 --> 00:03:18,938
The API changed, so know which version you're reading.

44
00:03:18,938 --> 00:03:23,603
Java 21 constructed a subclass like ShutdownOnFailure.

45
00:03:23,603 --> 00:03:26,540
Java 25 uses a static open method.

46
00:03:26,990 --> 00:03:35,161
The policy moved from choosing a subclass to passing a Joiner, so common policies are values rather than types you extend.

47
00:03:35,611 --> 00:03:39,822
In Java 21 you called join, then throwIfFailed.

48
00:03:39,822 --> 00:03:42,779
In Java 25, join throws directly.

49
00:03:43,229 --> 00:03:48,292
And ShutdownOnSuccess became anySuccessfulResultOrThrow.

50
00:03:48,292 --> 00:03:53,085
Java 21 code using the old shape won't compile on 25.

51
00:03:53,535 --> 00:04:02,717
As a preview feature, it needs enable-preview at compile and run time, and the class files only load on that exact JDK release.

52
00:04:02,717 --> 00:04:06,911
That alone is a reason to keep it out of shared libraries.

53
00:04:07,811 --> 00:04:10,137
The API enforces the structure.

54
00:04:10,137 --> 00:04:15,315
Calling get before join throws, because the result may not exist yet.

55
00:04:15,315 --> 00:04:17,266
Forking after join throws.

56
00:04:17,266 --> 00:04:20,568
And only the owner thread can fork and join.

57
00:04:21,018 --> 00:04:22,595
These aren't arbitrary.

58
00:04:22,595 --> 00:04:26,161
Each one closes off a way the structure could break.

59
00:04:26,161 --> 00:04:33,498
And scopes nest, so cancelling an outer scope cancels every inner scope, transitively, with no bookkeeping.

60
00:04:34,398 --> 00:04:40,176
Each scope records its owner and its subtasks, so the runtime sees a real tree.

61
00:04:40,176 --> 00:04:48,734
A JSON thread dump groups virtual threads by scope, giving you a call tree instead of ten thousand unrelated entries.

62
00:04:49,184 --> 00:04:51,224
Cancellation is interruption.

63
00:04:51,224 --> 00:04:55,164
So subtasks must respond to interruption for it to work.

64
00:04:55,164 --> 00:05:01,073
One stuck in a non-interruptible native call won't stop, and close will wait for it.

65
00:05:01,073 --> 00:05:04,309
Cooperative cancellation is still cooperative.

66
00:05:04,759 --> 00:05:07,418
And scoped values flow into subtasks.

67
00:05:07,418 --> 00:05:14,171
A binding made before opening the scope is inherited by every forked subtask, with no copying.

68
00:05:14,171 --> 00:05:15,895
That's the next chapter.

69
00:05:16,795 --> 00:05:18,160
Production rules.

70
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:21,209
Use scopes for request-scoped fan-out.

71
00:05:21,209 --> 00:05:28,833
Use allSuccessfulOrThrow when every input is required, and awaitAll for required plus optional.

72
00:05:28,833 --> 00:05:31,080
Keep subtasks interruptible.

73
00:05:31,080 --> 00:05:33,969
And verify the API against your JDK.

74
00:05:34,419 --> 00:05:38,227
Never use this preview API in shared libraries.

75
00:05:38,227 --> 00:05:41,468
Never copy Java 21 samples onto Java 25.

76
00:05:41,468 --> 00:05:46,492
Never call get before join, or pass a scope to another thread.

77
00:05:46,492 --> 00:05:52,893
And never swallow interruption inside a subtask, or cancellation stops working.

78
00:05:53,793 --> 00:05:54,462
To recap.

79
00:05:54,462 --> 00:06:00,105
A scope is a block with a lifetime, so subtasks can't be orphaned or leaked.

80
00:06:00,105 --> 00:06:05,822
A failure cancels the siblings, and join surfaces it where you can handle it.

81
00:06:05,822 --> 00:06:07,381
Policies are Joiners.

82
00:06:07,381 --> 00:06:12,801
And it's still a preview API, which changed shape between Java 21 and 25.

83
00:06:13,251 --> 00:06:15,659
That's the last chapter in this batch.

84
00:06:15,659 --> 00:06:20,347
The concurrency section finishes with chapter thirty-three, scoped values.

85
00:06:20,347 --> 00:06:21,614
Thanks for watching.
